---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { COMPANY } from '../data/apps';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About — ${COMPANY.name}`}
  description="Nintek is a one-person studio built around a real life — an SRE career, a woodworking hobby, a health journey, daily games, and keeping a home."
  path="/about"
>
  <section class="about-hero container reveal">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <div class="hero-text">
        <span class="eyebrow">
          <span class="eyebrow-dot"></span>
          The maker behind the apps
        </span>
        <h1>
          Built for the life
          <span class="em">I'm actually living.</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="about-lead">
          I'm an SRE by trade, a woodworker by weekend, and someone who went
          looking for software that fit my actual life — not a sanitized version
          of it. When I couldn't find it, I built it. That's Nintek.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-photo">
        <img src="/maker.png" alt="Enzo, the maker behind Nintek" width="220" height="220" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="container">
    <div class="about-story">

      <section class="story-section reveal">
        <span class="story-eyebrow">Work</span>
        <h2>An SRE who lives in terminals and docs.</h2>
        <p>
          My day job is keeping systems running. That means notes — a lot of
          them. Incident timelines, runbook tweaks, things I figured out at
          2am that I absolutely cannot lose. I tried every notes app and none
          of them felt right: too cluttered, too eager to sync everything to
          someone else's cloud, too slow to open when I'm already in the
          middle of something.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/tabloom">Tabloom</a> started as a personal experiment in what
          notes could feel like if speed and privacy came first. <a href="/pulsewire">PulseWire</a>
          came from a similar frustration — staying current in tech is part of
          the job, but the signal-to-noise ratio on the internet is brutal. And
          <a href="/cairn">Cairn</a>? Certifications don't stop being important
          once you're senior. If anything, they matter more. I built Cairn to
          keep my study habit alive without turning it into a second job.
        </p>
        <div class="story-apps">
          <a href="/tabloom" class="story-app-pill">Tabloom</a>
          <a href="/pulsewire" class="story-app-pill">PulseWire</a>
          <a href="/cairn" class="story-app-pill">Cairn</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section reveal">
        <span class="story-eyebrow">Craft</span>
        <h2>A hobbyist with a disorganized shop.</h2>
        <p>
          I've been doing woodworking long enough to have a real shop — table
          saw, router table, the works. And long enough to know the frustration
          of starting a project without being able to answer basic questions:
          do I have enough walnut for this? Where did I put the pocket hole
          jig? What were the measurements on that last cut list?
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/workshop">Workshop</a> is a build log — projects, cut lists,
          material counts, the notes I used to scribble on scrap wood and then
          lose. <a href="/shopkeep">ShopKeep</a> is the inventory layer: what's
          in the shop, where it lives, and when I'm running low on something.
          Together they're the shop foreman I never had.
        </p>
        <div class="story-apps">
          <a href="/workshop" class="story-app-pill">Workshop</a>
          <a href="/shopkeep" class="story-app-pill">ShopKeep</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section reveal">
        <span class="story-eyebrow">Health</span>
        <h2>When the numbers started to matter.</h2>
        <p>
          A while back, I started a new health chapter — the kind where you
          suddenly have metrics to track that you'd never paid attention to
          before. Weight trends, doses, how you're actually feeling week over
          week. I went looking for an app to help me stay on top of it and
          found the same thing everywhere: designs that felt clinical, privacy
          policies that read like a data harvest, and UX clearly made for
          someone who wasn't me.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/tare">Tare</a> is the app I wished I had. It tracks the metrics
          that matter on a GLP-1 journey, smooths out the daily noise so you
          can see the actual trend, and keeps everything local. No accounts.
          No cloud sync. Your health data is yours.
        </p>
        <div class="story-apps">
          <a href="/tare" class="story-app-pill">Tare</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section reveal">
        <span class="story-eyebrow">Play</span>
        <h2>An avid gamer who needed a reset.</h2>
        <p>
          I've been playing games my whole life — consoles, PC, all of it.
          But there are days when a full session isn't in the cards. What I
          wanted was something to decompress with during lunch or after dinner
          that didn't ask for more time than I had — something that used a
          different part of my brain than a workday does.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/puzzlebox">Puzzlebox</a> started as eight daily games in one
          place — Sudoku, word puzzles, logic grids. It kept growing. There's a
          30-wave tower defense campaign now, a stock-trading sim running on real
          market data, themed slot machines, and a 3D room with a playable arcade
          cabinet in it. The kind of mental reset that fits in five minutes or
          fifty. Your call.
        </p>
        <div class="story-apps">
          <a href="/puzzlebox" class="story-app-pill">Puzzlebox</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section reveal">
        <span class="story-eyebrow">Home</span>
        <h2>The household that runs on lost receipts.</h2>
        <p>
          I cook, I do my own maintenance, and I've got a property with
          opinions about when things need attention. The problem is the paper
          trail — or the lack of one. Where's the HVAC filter size? When was
          the last time the water heater was serviced? What was in that
          marinade everyone loved?
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/hearth">Hearth</a> is a household records hub. Appliances and
          their maintenance history, home documents, cooking notes — the stuff
          that's currently spread across your junk drawer, your email, and
          your fading memory. It has since grown a garden planner with a
          to-scale bed layout and a harvest log, and a corner that watches the
          home infrastructure I'd otherwise forget about until it broke.
        </p>
        <div class="story-apps">
          <a href="/hearth" class="story-app-pill">Hearth</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section story-native reveal">
        <span class="story-eyebrow">Direction</span>
        <h2>Then the apps started leaving the browser.</h2>
        <p>
          Everything above was built for the web, and the web is a genuinely
          good place for most of it. But I kept running into the same wall.
          Cairn's whole point is studying in the gaps of a day, and a browser
          tab can't put your streak on a Home Screen. Workshop is used standing
          at a table saw, where a Lock Screen checklist beats a laptop every
          time. ShopKeep spent years asking me to type in numbers that were
          printed on the box in a barcode.
        </p>
        <p>
          So I learned SwiftUI and started porting. <a href="/cairn">Cairn</a>
          went first and went furthest — the native version has no sign-in and no
          server at all, with every question bundled into the app and progress
          living in your own iCloud.
          <a href="/shopkeep">ShopKeep</a> turned the camera into the filing
          system. <a href="/workshop">Workshop</a> moved its cut-plan optimiser
          onto the device, so the math that used to be a server round trip now
          happens in your hand.
        </p>
        <p>
          The interesting part wasn't any single app — it was what accumulated
          between them. A shared Swift package called NintekKit now holds the
          models, the API clients, the widget plumbing and the ported
          algorithms, so each port costs less than the one before it. That's the
          difference between a pile of apps and an ecosystem.
        </p>
        <p>
          And then there's <strong>Cortex</strong>, which broke the pattern. It's
          not a port of Puzzlebox — five puzzles came across, and three more got
          written from scratch for iOS, including one running on a game engine
          embedded inside the app. It has Game Center leaderboards, real haptics,
          no account, and no network. It's the most fun I've had building
          anything in a while.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/tare">Tare</a> came last, and that was deliberate. It holds
          years of my own health data and I open it every day, so it was the one
          port I could not afford to get wrong — the other three were the
          practice. It went furthest in one particular way: the native version
          has no account at all, where the web app signs into Microsoft. Every
          weight, dose, glucose reading and lab result lives on the phone and
          syncs through my own iCloud, and the whole history came across in a
          single backup file.
        </p>
        <p>All five are in TestFlight beta now.</p>
        <div class="story-apps">
          <a href="/ios" class="story-app-pill story-app-pill--strong">
            The native apps →
          </a>
          <a href="/ios#cairn" class="story-app-pill">Cairn</a>
          <a href="/ios#shopkeep" class="story-app-pill">ShopKeep</a>
          <a href="/ios#workshop" class="story-app-pill">Workshop</a>
          <a href="/ios#cortex" class="story-app-pill">Cortex</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-close reveal">
        <p>
          Nintek is a one-person studio. Every app is private by default,
          built to last, and made because I needed it first. If any of this
          resonates — or if you've got something you're missing —
          <a href={`mailto:${COMPANY.email}`}>get in touch</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-hero {
    padding-top: 80px;
    padding-bottom: 72px;
  }

  .hero-inner {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 3rem;
    max-width: 72ch;
  }

  .hero-text {
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .hero-photo {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }

  .hero-photo img {
    width: 198px;
    height: 198px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center top;
    border: 3px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 30%, transparent);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-float);
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .hero-inner {
      flex-direction: column-reverse;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: 1.5rem;
    }
    .hero-photo img {
      width: 132px;
      height: 132px;
    }
  }

  .about-hero h1 {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.25rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .about-hero h1 .em {
    color: var(--copper);
    display: block;
  }

  .about-lead {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .about-story {
    max-width: 68ch;
    padding-bottom: 120px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0;
  }

  .story-section {
    padding-top: 56px;
    padding-bottom: 56px;
    border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 10%, transparent);
  }

  .story-eyebrow {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--copper);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .story-section h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 3vw, 1.75rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .story-section p {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.72;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .story-section p:last-of-type {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .story-section p a {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    text-decoration-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 40%, transparent);
    transition: text-decoration-color 0.15s;
  }

  .story-section p a:hover {
    text-decoration-color: var(--copper);
  }

  .story-apps {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .story-app-pill {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: 0.3rem 0.75rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 10%, transparent);
    color: var(--copper);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 25%, transparent);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: background 0.15s, border-color 0.15s;
  }

  .story-app-pill:hover {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 18%, transparent);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--copper) 45%, transparent);
  }

  .story-app-pill--strong {
    background: var(--copper);
    color: #fff;
    border-color: var(--copper);
  }

  .story-app-pill--strong:hover {
    background: var(--accent);
    border-color: var(--accent);
  }

  .story-native p strong {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .story-close {
    padding-top: 56px;
    border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 10%, transparent);
  }

  .story-close p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.72;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .story-close a {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 500;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
  }
</style>
